# Tailwind follows the linter, not invented arbitrary values

Detect the installed Tailwind major version (`package.json`, `@import "tailwindcss"` vs `tailwind.config.*`). Write classes in that version's canonical form. After edits, fix Tailwind IntelliSense diagnostics (`suggestCanonicalClasses`, `cssConflict`) instead of leaving warnings.

## Do not use `-[]` when a canonical class exists

Arbitrary values are a last resort. If the linter can rewrite it, write the rewrite.

```
# Tailwind v4 (spacing is 0.25rem / 4px)
w-[236px] h-[76px]     →  w-59 h-19
h-[68px] w-[68px]      →  size-17
w-[16px]               →  w-4
bg-[var(--accent)]     →  bg-(--accent)
px-[var(--pad-x)]      →  px-(--pad-x)
z-[5]                  →  z-5
duration-[150ms]       →  duration-150
tracking-[0.1em]       →  tracking-widest
leading-[1.5]          →  leading-normal
bg-white/[0.02]        →  bg-white/2
```

v3 has a fixed spacing scale (`w-4`, `w-64`) and no `utility-(--var)` shorthand. Do not invent v4 scale numbers (`w-59`) in a v3 project.

## Arbitrary `-[]` is OK only when nothing else matches

Keep `max-w-[36ch]`, `grid-cols-[200px_1fr]`, `min-[601px]:`, `color-mix(...)`, `outline-offset-[-0.5px]`, and similar one-offs. Do not force a nearby token if it changes the design.

## Never

- Pixel arbitrary values that divide evenly by 4 in v4 (`-[Np x]` → scale)
- `-[var(--token)]` in v4 (use `-(--token)`)
- Ignoring the Problems panel after writing `className`
